    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they turn guesses into promises. A team identifies a problem, imagines a solution, estimates the work, and places a feature on a timeline. By the time that timeline reaches executives or customers, the uncertainty has disappeared. What began as “we think this might help” becomes “this will ship in September.”\n\nThat shift changes how teams behave. Instead of asking whether an idea is still worth pursuing, they ask how to deliver it on schedule. New evidence becomes an inconvenience. A customer interview that challenges the original assumption is treated as a threat to the plan rather than a reason to improve it. The roadmap starts protecting decisions that should still be open to question.\n\nMany roadmaps also confuse activity with progress. Launching a dashboard, rebuilding onboarding, or adding an integration sounds concrete, but none of those things is inherently valuable. Their value depends on what changes for users and the business. When success means shipping the promised feature, teams can complete the roadmap without solving the problems that justified it.\n\nThen there is the problem of competing interests. Sales wants features that close deals. Support wants fewer recurring complaints. Executives want visible moves into new markets. Engineering wants time to fix fragile systems. Each concern may be legitimate, but putting all of them on one timeline does not create a strategy. It often creates a compromise in which everything matters and nothing receives enough attention.\n\nThe calendar adds another distortion. Product work includes discovery, technical surprises, dependencies, and feedback that cannot be scheduled precisely months in advance. Yet roadmaps frequently present distant work with the same confidence as work already underway. Teams respond by padding estimates, cutting scope, or quietly sacrificing quality. The dates survive while the intended value shrinks.\n\nA useful roadmap makes choices visible without pretending the future is settled. It explains which problems matter, why they matter now, and what evidence would justify continuing, changing direction, or stopping. It distinguishes genuine commitments from possibilities and becomes less specific as it looks further ahead.\n\nThis does not mean abandoning deadlines or accountability. Some dates are real, and teams need to coordinate. But accountability should include noticing when a plan is wrong, not just executing it faithfully.\n\nMost roadmaps fail when people expect them to remove uncertainty rather than help manage it. A roadmap earns its place when it helps a team decide what to do next, including when to leave an old idea behind.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams cannot deliver, but because the roadmap promises the wrong kind of certainty. A document meant to guide decisions becomes a contract about the future. Features acquire deadlines before teams understand the problems, estimates harden into commitments, and success becomes shipping what was promised rather than creating value.\n\nThe first failure is confusing outputs with outcomes. “Launch a dashboard” is a deliverable; “help customers identify problems faster” is a goal. A roadmap organized around deliverables assumes the solution is already known. Once a feature appears on the timeline, questioning its usefulness can look like resistance or delay. Teams may execute perfectly and still produce something customers barely use. The roadmap is completed, but the problem remains.\n\nThe second failure is treating uncertainty as a planning defect. Product development involves discovering what customers need, what technology permits, and what the business can sustain. These discoveries change the work. Yet detailed, long-range roadmaps often conceal uncertainty rather than acknowledge it. A precise launch date can create confidence without creating knowledge. When reality diverges from the plan, teams are pressured to defend the forecast instead of updating it.\n\nOrganizational incentives make this worse. Sales wants commitments that help close deals. Executives want predictability. Customers want reassurance that their requests matter. Product leaders may accommodate all three by adding more items, creating a roadmap that reflects bargaining power rather than a coherent strategy. Everything becomes a priority because saying no carries an immediate political cost, while overcommitment creates consequences later.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they ignore how work actually happens. Maintenance, reliability improvements, technical debt, and unexpected dependencies compete with planned features for the same limited capacity. If the roadmap assumes uninterrupted delivery, ordinary operational demands become apparent failures. Teams respond by cutting quality, shrinking scope, or quietly moving dates—each of which weakens trust.\n\nThe alternative is not to abandon planning. Teams still need direction, coordination, and accountability. But a useful roadmap distinguishes commitments from hypotheses. It connects proposed work to measurable outcomes, makes trade-offs explicit, and becomes less detailed as the time horizon extends. Near-term commitments can be concrete; distant opportunities should remain conditional on evidence.\n\nAbove all, a roadmap needs a revision process. Customer research, experiments, and delivery experience should be allowed to change priorities without being treated as excuses. Accountability should include explaining what was learned and why the plan changed, not merely whether the original schedule survived.\n\nA roadmap succeeds when it helps an organization make better decisions as circumstances change. It fails when preserving the plan matters more than achieving the purpose behind it.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
